course: dictionary
lesson: 11907
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word buoy is a good example. «The sailor steered the boat past the red buoy.» Here a buoy is a floating object in the water that marks a safe path or a danger.
explain: «She threw a life buoy to the boy who had fallen into the river.» This time a buoy is a floating ring thrown to someone in trouble. Look at the clues around it: thrown, fallen in, river. Those clues tell you which meaning to choose.
explain: Buoy can also be a verb, a doing word. «The good news buoyed her up and she kept going.» Buoyed up means made more cheerful or hopeful. Check by swapping in your meaning: "The good news made her more hopeful" still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: A buoy is a floating object that stays in one place in the sea or a river.
fact: Buoys can mark a safe path, a danger, or a place to stop.
fact: A life buoy is a floating ring thrown to someone who is in the water.
fact: «Buoy» can also be a verb meaning to keep something afloat, or to make someone feel more cheerful.
fact: Buoys float because of buoyancy, the force that keeps things floating in water.
exercise: «The swimmer stayed between the flags, away from the buoys.» What is a buoy here? | A flag on the sand | *A floating marker in the water | A big wave | A type of small boat
exercise: «Throw the life buoy to the man in the water!» What is a life buoy? | A swimming costume | A lifeguard's whistle | A heavy anchor for a boat | *A floating ring thrown to someone in trouble
exercise: «The kind words buoyed her up after a sad day.» What does buoyed up mean? | *Made her feel more cheerful and hopeful | Made her feel cold | Made her go to sleep | Pushed her under the water
test: Buoys float because of which science idea? | *Buoyancy | Gravity makes them fall | Magnets | Sand on the seabed
test: «The coastguard placed a marker buoy near the rocks.» What does a marker buoy warn about? | A kind of boat engine | A toy for children to play with | A bird that lives on rocks | *A danger in the water
exam: In «Stay between the flags and away from the buoys», what is a buoy? | A kind of hat | A large, dry stone | *A floating object that marks a safe path or a danger | A fast car
